6
00:00:19,279 --> 00:00:21,590
Welcome to Software Engineering Radio.

7
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,390
I'm your host Gregory Caphammer.

8
00:00:24,390 --> 00:00:27,029
Today's guest is Casper Wakovski.

9
00:00:27,029 --> 00:00:29,589
He's a senior developer advocate at Quadrant.

10
00:00:29,589 --> 00:00:34,150
Quadrant is an open- source vector database and vector search similarity search engine.

11
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:35,910
Casper, welcome to the show.

12
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:37,911
Hello, Greg. Thanks for the invitation.

13
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:41,590
Hey, I'm really glad today that we get a chance to talk about Quadrant.

14
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:46,389
It's a vector database and we're going to learn more about how it helps us to solve a.

15
00:00:46,399 --> 00:00:49,350
number of key problems. So, are you ready to dive in?

16
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:51,670
Definitely. Okay.

17
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:53,590
So, we're going to start with an introduction to vector databases.

18
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:59,270
and we're going to cover a couple highle concepts and then later dive into some additional details.

19
00:00:59,270 --> 00:01:03,111
So let's start with the simple question of what is a vector database? Can you tell us more?

20
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:05,270
Yes, of course.

21
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:09,270
First of all, I think vector search engine is a more appropriate term.

22
00:01:09,270 --> 00:01:13,429
A search is the main functionality that this kind of tools provide.

23
00:01:13,429 --> 00:01:21,270
Nevertheless, it's a service that can efficiently store and handle highdimensional vectors for the purposes of similarity search.

24
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:26,550
And similarity of the vectors is defined by the closeness of the vectors in that space.

25
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:29,670
So vector databases are built to make that process efficient.

26
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:32,230
Okay.

27
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:37,429
So a vector database helps us to achieve vector search or vector similarity search. Is that the right way to think about it?

28
00:01:37,439 --> 00:01:39,670
Exactly. Okay.

29
00:01:39,670 --> 00:01:43,350
Now one of the things you mentioned was the word vector and then you said highdimensional.

30
00:01:43,350 --> 00:01:46,630
Can you briefly explain what highdimensional data is?

31
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:48,710
Yes.

32
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:50,310
In case of vector embeddings, we describe them as high dimensional.

33
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:54,710
because they usually have at least a few hundreds of dimensions.

34
00:01:54,710 --> 00:01:58,469
Typically, no more than eight or 9,000 dimensions.

35
00:01:58,469 --> 00:02:04,789
And it's definitely not like high dimensional data if you are seasoned data expert,

36
00:02:04,789 --> 00:02:09,430
but it's relatively high because it's hard to imagine, hard to interpret for a regular human.

37
00:02:09,430 --> 00:02:11,910
So, this is the range that we are usually operating in.

38
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:13,910
Okay, that's helpful.

39
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:16,070
Now, you mentioned the term embedding a moment ago.

40
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:19,589
Can you talk briefly about the concept of a vector embedding?

41
00:02:19,599 --> 00:02:21,670
Sure.

42
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:27,430
So, vector embeddings are just numerical representations of the input data.

43
00:02:27,430 --> 00:02:29,750
And the main idea is that they keep the semantic meaning of the input data.

44
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:31,990
that was used to generate them.

45
00:02:31,990 --> 00:02:37,190
And if we have two different vectors which are similar in some way.

46
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:42,229
Then we assume that the objects that are used to generate them are also similar in their nature.

47
00:02:42,239 --> 00:02:48,390
And vector embeddings actually enabled semantic search that can understand not only the presence of particular keywords,

48
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:51,509
but also user intents.

49
00:02:51,509 --> 00:02:59,270
And more importantly they enabled search on unstructured data that was impossible to be processed in the past.

50
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:03,190
So let me see if I'm understanding the workflow correctly.

51
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:05,670
is the idea that I take something like source code.

52
00:03:05,670 --> 00:03:10,869
or images or documents and then I convert those to embeddings.

53
00:03:10,869 --> 00:03:13,190
and then I store those in the vector database.

54
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:15,110
Am I thinking about this the right way?

55
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:19,509
Yes, that's the correct way and the main idea is.

56
00:03:19,509 --> 00:03:24,470
that this vector embeddings are generated by neural networks which were trained solely for that purposes.

57
00:03:24,470 --> 00:03:29,350
So that's also why we quite often describe vector search as neural search.

58
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:35,910
because it requires some sort of neural networks to encode the data into this numerical representations.

59
00:03:35,910 --> 00:03:40,229
Some of our listeners may not have previously used a vector database.

60
00:03:40,229 --> 00:03:42,309
or done some type of vector similarity search.

61
00:03:42,309 --> 00:03:44,070
Can you tell us a little bit more about how you know.

62
00:03:44,070 --> 00:03:48,630
when your project actually needs a vector database?

63
00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:50,550
There are no strict criteria here of course.

64
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:53,350
But generally if you build any kind of search mechanism.

65
00:03:53,350 --> 00:03:57,830
and whenever you want to add this semantic search capabilities into it.

66
00:03:57,830 --> 00:03:59,509
Then you should have a look at vector databases.

67
00:03:59,519 --> 00:04:06,229
Because they just make the deployment and the maintenance of this kind of projects easier.

68
00:04:06,229 --> 00:04:09,509
Also, when you want to implement search over some data modality.

69
00:04:09,519 --> 00:04:15,030
that can be processed with traditional search means such as images or audio.

70
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:19,430
Then you definitely need to use semantic search.

71
00:04:19,430 --> 00:04:24,070
Because that's probably the only approach to search on unstructured data like this.

72
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:28,710
And of course, if you have just a few examples of documents that never change,

73
00:04:28,710 --> 00:04:33,990
then vector databases might be just an additional overhead in your project.

74
00:04:33,990 --> 00:04:38,230
So then maybe implementing a semantic search directly into your application.

75
00:04:38,230 --> 00:04:43,431
and embedding this documents directly into the source code makes sense. But in general if you have data.

76
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:51,749
that changes frequently you should be using vector database to implement semantic search.

77
00:04:51,749 --> 00:04:56,310
Especially nowadays vector databases come along well with large language models.

78
00:04:56,310 --> 00:05:01,990
Because in both cases we expect natural language like interactions.

79
00:05:01,990 --> 00:05:07,029
And we are not necessarily looking only at the presence of the keywords.

80
00:05:07,029 --> 00:05:10,629
So if you build a system that exposes a conversational like interface.

81
00:05:10,639 --> 00:05:15,830
then vector databases might be really important to achieve that quickly.

82
00:05:15,830 --> 00:05:20,070
So you mentioned the idea of keyword search engine.

83
00:05:20,070 --> 00:05:23,749
and we've already talked about the concept of a similarity search engine.

84
00:05:23,749 --> 00:05:28,469
How are these two types of search engines similar to and different from each other?

85
00:05:28,469 --> 00:05:31,510
So historically search was tied solely to textual data.

86
00:05:31,510 --> 00:05:36,790
We didn't have any other means that would allow us to search over images.

87
00:05:36,790 --> 00:05:39,110
or any different data modality.

88
00:05:39,110 --> 00:05:41,830
And since we are only focusing on text,

89
00:05:41,830 --> 00:05:49,029
we developed some specific methods that were dividing the text into meaningful pieces, not necessarily specific words.

90
00:05:49,029 --> 00:05:56,629
But we were also converting them into their root forms through stemming or some different leatization techniques.

91
00:05:56,629 --> 00:05:58,950
And then we were just building inverted indexes.

92
00:05:58,950 --> 00:06:05,990
that were supporting the lexical search which was based on the present on some specific keywords.

93
00:06:05,990 --> 00:06:08,070
And imagine you had a very specific use case in.

94
00:06:08,070 --> 00:06:12,790
which two different words could describe the same object, the same phenomena.

95
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:15,909
Then you would need to manually maintain a list of synonyms.

96
00:06:15,919 --> 00:06:20,870
So this process will convert all the different synonyms into the same form.

97
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:23,270
So that means a lot of effort,

98
00:06:23,270 --> 00:06:29,670
maybe even building a whole team of people focusing on search and improving search relevance.

99
00:06:29,670 --> 00:06:34,230
And semantic search is slightly different because it based on neural networks.

100
00:06:34,230 --> 00:06:40,870
And this neural networks are trained to understand the meaning of the words and whole sentences.

101
00:06:40,870 --> 00:06:45,749
And that means you don't necessarily need to use the same terminology as the people.

102
00:06:45,749 --> 00:06:47,749
who created the documents you are searching over.

103
00:06:47,759 --> 00:06:51,990
But you can also express yourself however you want,

104
00:06:51,990 --> 00:06:56,790
assuming the model was trained properly for that particular language.

105
00:06:56,790 --> 00:06:59,110
And still get significantly better results even.

106
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:06,070
though you can't really speak the same language as the domain experts who created the whole database.

107
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:10,309
So that's the main difference and also historically we were using tools such as elastic search.

108
00:07:10,309 --> 00:07:15,110
or open search or anything based on lucine actually to support lexical search.

109
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:20,390
Right now, vector databases are just another different search paradigm.

110
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:23,350
Thanks. That response was really helpful.

111
00:07:23,350 --> 00:07:25,510
So, I want to turn our attention now to Quadrant.

112
00:07:25,510 --> 00:07:31,110
and then briefly discuss some of the types of applications people can build with Quadrant.

113
00:07:31,110 --> 00:07:34,950
So, at the start of the show, you said that Quadrant was a vector similarity search engine.

114
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:36,710
Can you tell us a few of the key features.

115
00:07:36,710 --> 00:07:40,790
that Quadrant provides and what developers can actually build with Quadrant?

116
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,430
Of course.

117
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:45,990
So quadrant provides a very efficient and lightweight search engine.

118
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:53,589
that can handle different types of vectors dense sparse and multi vector representations.

119
00:07:53,589 --> 00:07:59,029
And we also support all the existing optimization techniques which are relevant for that space.

120
00:07:59,029 --> 00:08:05,750
So just to name a few we support different kinds of quantization such as product scalar. and binary quantization.

121
00:08:05,750 --> 00:08:13,270
That helps to reduce the amount of memory required to run semantic search at scale.

122
00:08:13,270 --> 00:08:17,110
We can also store the data on disk.

123
00:08:17,110 --> 00:08:19,110
if you prefer to reduce the cost of running semantic search.

124
00:08:19,110 --> 00:08:23,749
And you are okay with higher latency or GPU based indexing.

125
00:08:23,749 --> 00:08:28,070
If you really care about the time spent on building some helpful data structures.

126
00:08:28,070 --> 00:08:31,189
that we use to make that search efficient.

127
00:08:31,189 --> 00:08:33,029
And one important feature or functionality of quiron is.

128
00:08:33,039 --> 00:08:38,070
that it allows to keep multiple vectors per each point along with some metadata.

129
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:39,990
that can be also used for filtering.

130
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:46,949
Which is kind of important because a typical use case requires you not only to search based on.

131
00:08:46,949 --> 00:08:48,949
the semantics of the data.

132
00:08:48,949 --> 00:08:50,630
Imagine you are looking for the best restaurants nearby you.

133
00:08:50,630 --> 00:08:54,470
You definitely don't want to see restaurants from the other part of the globe.

134
00:08:54,470 --> 00:08:58,630
You definitely want to restrict your search to a specific area.

135
00:08:58,630 --> 00:09:00,470
So you don't need to travel to have your dinner.

136
00:09:00,470 --> 00:09:05,509
And that's exactly where metadata filtering is important.

137
00:09:05,509 --> 00:09:09,750
And it's implemented in a slightly unique way compared to the other vector databases.

138
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:12,389
So I would say those are the main features of quadrant.

139
00:09:12,399 --> 00:09:21,430
and when it comes to different applications what quadrant implements is actually an approximation of nearest neighbor search.

140
00:09:21,430 --> 00:09:26,070
KN&amp;N K nearest newest neighbors is a quite well-known algorithm for those.

141
00:09:26,070 --> 00:09:28,230
who have any kind of experience in machine learning.

142
00:09:28,230 --> 00:09:36,070
That's probably the most basic ML algorithm that exists and it's known for its versatility.

143
00:09:36,070 --> 00:09:38,870
However, it's really hard to scale it up just.

144
00:09:38,870 --> 00:09:47,510
because there at inference time KN&amp;N requires us to compare the distance to all the vectors we have. within the system.

145
00:09:47,510 --> 00:09:54,550
So quadrant as well all the other vector databases just approximate nearest neighbor search.

146
00:09:54,550 --> 00:09:57,590
So it's can be implemented in sublinear time.

147
00:09:57,590 --> 00:10:01,110
But that also means that we can solve variety of problems.

148
00:10:01,110 --> 00:10:05,990
that pure KN&amp;N could also solve obviously semantic search.

149
00:10:05,990 --> 00:10:07,750
So if you have an existing application.

150
00:10:07,750 --> 00:10:14,069
and just want to enhance it with additional semantic search capabilities that's something you could definitely implement with.

151
00:10:14,079 --> 00:10:17,269
Quadrant.

152
00:10:17,269 --> 00:10:19,670
However, vector search enables way more than just pure search.

153
00:10:19,670 --> 00:10:22,150
Cuz since we have the similarity measure,

154
00:10:22,150 --> 00:10:29,990
we can also perform a very simple classification pipeline using the same approach.

155
00:10:29,990 --> 00:10:36,230
Because if we just select the top 10 closest documents or top 10 closest documents in general.

156
00:10:36,230 --> 00:10:43,431
By running a simple voting procedure, we can just select the most common class among all these closest. neighbors.

157
00:10:43,431 --> 00:10:45,750
And assign the class to a new observation.

158
00:10:45,750 --> 00:10:50,630
Just because the majority of observations in its neighborhood belong to it.

159
00:10:50,630 --> 00:10:55,350
And the similarity measure is also interesting on its own just.

160
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:58,389
because you can use it to detect anomalies.

161
00:10:58,389 --> 00:11:02,870
Let's say you know the distribution of the queries you typically get into your system.

162
00:11:02,870 --> 00:11:11,829
Then you can also detect that a particular query is just way below the expected range of similarity.

163
00:11:11,829 --> 00:11:16,230
And then maybe add a human in the loop component just to react to that particular observation.

164
00:11:16,230 --> 00:11:21,110
Because that may indicate that somebody is just trying to hack your system for example.

165
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:25,910
And last but not least, recommendation engines.

166
00:11:25,910 --> 00:11:31,269
If you have positive and negative examples such as movies that somebody liked or disliked.

167
00:11:31,269 --> 00:11:34,310
You can also use multiple vectors.

168
00:11:34,310 --> 00:11:42,310
And serve recommendations based on this multiple objects that that particular person has interacted with in the past.

169
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:44,150
Thanks for that explanation.

170
00:11:44,150 --> 00:11:50,389
It was really helpful and I appreciated in particular you commenting on the idea of a recommendation engine.

171
00:11:50,389 --> 00:11:56,710
I wanted to think about recommendation maybe from a perspective that would be accessible for our listeners.

172
00:11:56,710 --> 00:12:01,030
So for example, if we're thinking about software testing and I have a test case that fails.

173
00:12:01,030 --> 00:12:06,550
How could I use semantic search to find the other test cases.

174
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:09,430
that are like the failing test case that might perhaps also fail.

175
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:13,030
So that I don't have to run the whole regression test suite?

176
00:12:13,030 --> 00:12:15,030
Can you walk us through that type of example?

177
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:18,790
Yeah, I can definitely try to describe the approach.

178
00:12:18,790 --> 00:12:20,870
However, I can't promise that it's going to work.

179
00:12:20,870 --> 00:12:23,350
But definitely there are some embedding models.

180
00:12:23,350 --> 00:12:28,389
that can work with uh source code different languages at the same time as well.

181
00:12:28,389 --> 00:12:35,590
And I can imagine that somebody could just encode all the test cases from the suits just to.

182
00:12:35,590 --> 00:12:39,509
capture the meaning of a particular test.

183
00:12:39,509 --> 00:12:41,829
And then if you have a let's say a quadrant instance.

184
00:12:41,829 --> 00:12:47,750
and a collection with all these representations of all the test cases you have.

185
00:12:47,750 --> 00:12:52,550
Then you can try to find for the nearest neighbors of failing test case that you just encountered.

186
00:12:52,550 --> 00:12:56,949
And then try to run them first to evaluate whether they are also failing.

187
00:12:56,949 --> 00:12:59,269
So that might one of the approaches to that.

188
00:12:59,269 --> 00:13:07,430
And since you would be using an embedding model that was trained solely to support code search.

189
00:13:07,430 --> 00:13:09,509
Then I would expect it to work properly.

190
00:13:09,509 --> 00:13:13,750
Just because the nature of the code is slightly different from natural language like processing.

191
00:13:13,750 --> 00:13:21,269
Cuz here it's not only about the convention of how we name our variables methods and classes.

192
00:13:21,269 --> 00:13:25,190
But it's more about the structure and the syntax of the code itself.

193
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:27,430
So this kind of model should capture more nuances of the data.

194
00:13:27,430 --> 00:13:33,030
and hopefully recognize this problematic test cases early on.

195
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:35,269
Okay, that makes sense.

196
00:13:35,269 --> 00:13:39,350
So in this case, I have to find the source code of the test cases.

197
00:13:39,350 --> 00:13:43,991
And then I have to produce an embedding of the source code of the test cases. Store that inside a quadrant.

198
00:13:43,991 --> 00:13:50,710
And then use it to help me to find the k nearest neighbors associated with that test case. Am I thinking about that the right way?

199
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:52,310
Exactly.

200
00:13:52,310 --> 00:13:54,310
That's the process I would suggest to test.

201
00:13:54,310 --> 00:13:58,069
Unfortunately, that's an interesting use case, but I haven't tried it on my own yet.

202
00:13:58,079 --> 00:13:59,910
Okay.

203
00:13:59,910 --> 00:14:03,350
I wanted to talk briefly about some of the other use cases that you mentioned a moment ago.

204
00:14:03,350 --> 00:14:10,629
So, you might want to do semantic search for like documentation, maybe markdown files or PDFs.

205
00:14:10,629 --> 00:14:13,670
What do you have to do to put the markdown file.

206
00:14:13,670 --> 00:14:16,150
or the PDF in the right format before you embed it?

207
00:14:16,150 --> 00:14:21,509
Can you talk a little bit further about how to do semantic search for various types of documents?

208
00:14:21,519 --> 00:14:23,350
Of course.

209
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:30,151
So markdown files are actually the easiest case cuz here we have just text with some additional formatting. apply on top of that.

210
00:14:30,151 --> 00:14:37,751
And the main challenge here is that we can't easily just put a whole document into an embedding. model.

211
00:14:37,751 --> 00:14:42,230
And expect it to encode the whole meaning of that document within a few hundreds of dimensions.

212
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:46,230
That would be like a perfect compression mechanism.

213
00:14:46,230 --> 00:14:49,509
if you can just put a whole book inside of such a short vector.

214
00:14:49,509 --> 00:14:54,790
So definitely what we need to do is to chunk.

215
00:14:54,790 --> 00:14:59,189
that into meaningful pieces and the way we chunk really depends on the data we have.

216
00:14:59,189 --> 00:15:01,430
As we are speaking about markdown files,

217
00:15:01,430 --> 00:15:07,509
you typically have some headers and paragraphs at least maybe some list tables etc.

218
00:15:07,509 --> 00:15:12,389
So if you want to chant your markdown files properly to bring all the context possible.

219
00:15:12,389 --> 00:15:18,870
Then you probably need to take all the headers way down to the particular paragraph you're encoding.

220
00:15:18,870 --> 00:15:24,550
Just to keep the traction of all the headers that have appeared so far.

221
00:15:24,550 --> 00:15:27,430
So then you are building more trust.

222
00:15:27,430 --> 00:15:29,990
that the embedding will capture all the information.

223
00:15:29,990 --> 00:15:33,990
that it has to capture in order to keep the meaning of that particular piece.

224
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,750
However, it's pretty tricky.

225
00:15:35,750 --> 00:15:40,310
Like there is no single method that you can use for chunking.

226
00:15:40,310 --> 00:15:43,509
The naive way of just using a fixed window length doesn't usually work.

227
00:15:43,519 --> 00:15:48,949
Because chunking itself like imagine you are reading a book.

228
00:15:48,949 --> 00:15:51,189
And if you just start from a random paragraph.

229
00:15:51,189 --> 00:15:53,350
It's really hard to say what was the meaning of.

230
00:15:53,350 --> 00:15:58,790
that paragraph in the context of a whole book or just a chapter.

231
00:15:58,790 --> 00:16:00,870
So chunking usually requires some additional means.

