Which is going to allow me to like extract the data from my documents,

689
00:46:35,520 --> 00:46:39,030
maybe using chunking like you mentioned a moment ago.

690
00:46:39,030 --> 00:46:45,911
And then I actually have to put it into the quadrant vector similarity search engine that's running in. my server.

691
00:46:45,911 --> 00:46:48,950
So I know that there are Python Go.

692
00:46:48,950 --> 00:46:51,030
and Rust libraries that are helping people to build the clients.

693
00:46:51,030 --> 00:46:57,349
Can you talk a little bit more about how developers would use these client libraries to interact with. the Quadrant server?

694
00:46:57,359 --> 00:46:59,270
Of course.

695
00:46:59,270 --> 00:47:04,630
So all of these clients are actually some interfaces built on top of HTTP.

696
00:47:04,630 --> 00:47:09,349
and gRPC protocols because that's what Quadrant exposes in the first place.

697
00:47:09,349 --> 00:47:11,910
However, the most popular client is our Python SDK.

698
00:47:11,910 --> 00:47:13,589
And it comes with some additional benefits.

699
00:47:13,589 --> 00:47:19,270
Because you can interact with both protocols using the same interfaces.

700
00:47:19,270 --> 00:47:21,589
And that's fine if you let's say have some restrictions.

701
00:47:21,589 --> 00:47:25,670
And let's say at this point you can't use the gRPC protocol.

702
00:47:25,670 --> 00:47:30,150
Because it's just not allowed in the network you are operating on.

703
00:47:30,150 --> 00:47:34,710
Then you can still use quadrant in the HTTP mode and eventually switch to gRPC.

704
00:47:34,710 --> 00:47:37,510
Because it's just a bit more efficient once this is all solved.

705
00:47:37,510 --> 00:47:43,430
So those libraries are actually some thin wrappers around our HTTP and gRPC APIs.

706
00:47:43,440 --> 00:47:48,069
That just makes things a bit easier cuz we take care of the batching.

707
00:47:48,069 --> 00:47:50,390
When you insert your data with our clients,

708
00:47:50,390 --> 00:47:56,630
you can expect them to just send them in batches and that's a good practice to do that.

709
00:47:56,630 --> 00:48:01,190
Retries might be handled automatically, but overall you will be calling the methods.

710
00:48:01,190 --> 00:48:05,910
which are named similarly to the HTTP endpoints for example.

711
00:48:05,910 --> 00:48:08,230
So that's what you typically do and.

712
00:48:08,230 --> 00:48:10,470
that depends on on the language that you choose.

713
00:48:10,470 --> 00:48:14,790
because some of them may have synchronous and asynchronous versions of the methods.

714
00:48:14,790 --> 00:48:17,190
So so that really depends on the platform.

715
00:48:17,190 --> 00:48:19,910
but eventually you can also use HTTP.

716
00:48:19,910 --> 00:48:22,150
or gRPC protocols directly depending on the platform you are working with.

717
00:48:22,160 --> 00:48:23,829
Okay.

718
00:48:23,829 --> 00:48:26,549
So what you're saying is that whether I'm using Python Go.

719
00:48:26,549 --> 00:48:31,270
or Rust I have two protocol choices in terms of how I interact with the Quadrant server.

720
00:48:31,280 --> 00:48:36,150
Just very quickly, when I was using the Python client SDK myself.

721
00:48:36,150 --> 00:48:41,589
Essentially what I did was I created a virtual environment.

722
00:48:41,589 --> 00:48:46,390
And then used UV in order to install the Quadrant client as a dependency.

723
00:48:46,390 --> 00:48:50,150
What I also did next was actually use something like sentence transformers to create my embeddings.

724
00:48:50,160 --> 00:48:51,750
But just to make sure I'm clear,

725
00:48:51,760 --> 00:48:56,870
Quadrant doesn't technically care whether I use sentence transformers.

726
00:48:56,870 --> 00:49:02,391
or open AI or any other way to create my embeddings. Did I get that correct?

727
00:49:02,400 --> 00:49:04,230
Yes, that's totally right.

728
00:49:04,230 --> 00:49:08,230
Actually, we do not assume that you will be using a particular model to encode your data.

729
00:49:08,240 --> 00:49:14,230
Many of our users at some point decide to fine-tune their own models.

730
00:49:14,230 --> 00:49:18,549
So, so they reflect their domain a a little bit better.

731
00:49:18,549 --> 00:49:22,950
So like we can't be really supporting a very particular set of models, quadrant is model agnostic.

732
00:49:22,950 --> 00:49:27,270
So so no matter how you create your vectors as long as you can provide them as a.

733
00:49:27,280 --> 00:49:31,109
list of floats, it's fine to to use them with quadrant.

734
00:49:31,119 --> 00:49:32,791
Okay, thanks. That was awesome.

735
00:49:32,791 --> 00:49:34,790
So I can pick Python Go or Rust.

736
00:49:34,790 --> 00:49:38,630
I can pick from a very wide variety of embedding libraries.

737
00:49:38,630 --> 00:49:40,549
And then based on what you said just a moment ago.

738
00:49:40,549 --> 00:49:44,710
I can even do some fine-tuning of the embedding library for the specific type of data.

739
00:49:44,720 --> 00:49:47,349
that I care about.

740
00:49:47,349 --> 00:49:50,630
Like markdown files or source code or PDFs or other things of that nature.

741
00:49:50,640 --> 00:49:55,670
Yes, many of our users just start with some existing models.

742
00:49:55,670 --> 00:50:00,309
and then at some point they decide to fine-tune something for their own purposes. So yes, you can do it.

743
00:50:00,319 --> 00:50:01,829
All right, that's awesome.

744
00:50:01,829 --> 00:50:05,670
What I want to do now, since you mentioned the idea of fine-tuning.

745
00:50:05,670 --> 00:50:09,190
I'd like to talk a little bit about some of your experiences.

746
00:50:09,190 --> 00:50:11,270
that you and other members of the Quadrant team have had.

747
00:50:11,270 --> 00:50:17,589
When it comes to things like building or testing or doing performance evaluation for Quadrant.

748
00:50:17,589 --> 00:50:22,309
So, I wanted to start by asking you to share a story maybe of like a challenging bug.

749
00:50:22,309 --> 00:50:25,990
or performance issue that you faced when you were developing Quadrant.

750
00:50:25,990 --> 00:50:29,829
And then could you tell our listeners a little bit more about how you. and the team solved that issue?

751
00:50:29,839 --> 00:50:31,589
Of course.

752
00:50:31,589 --> 00:50:33,430
This is actually described in our website.

753
00:50:33,430 --> 00:50:35,990
We have a pretty nice article describing that.

754
00:50:35,990 --> 00:50:40,870
For those of you who have some Rust experience, you probably have heard about Rox DB.

755
00:50:40,870 --> 00:50:44,150
Which is embedded key value store.

756
00:50:44,150 --> 00:50:49,190
And it has been a key cornerstone for us to persist mutable data on disk for a pretty.

757
00:50:49,190 --> 00:50:51,511
long time. It has one major problem though.

758
00:50:51,520 --> 00:50:54,470
It requires periodic compaction of data.

759
00:50:54,480 --> 00:51:00,069
That also means we need to do some sort of housekeeping to structure the data.

760
00:51:00,079 --> 00:51:03,109
and to drop some old data for example.

761
00:51:03,119 --> 00:51:09,109
So whenever this compaction job runs it can block everything else.

762
00:51:09,109 --> 00:51:14,230
and cause some latency spikes similarly to the garbage collector and we had no control over it.

763
00:51:14,240 --> 00:51:21,030
So that's also why we decided to implement something different a custom key value store.

764
00:51:21,030 --> 00:51:23,109
which we called greet store.

765
00:51:23,109 --> 00:51:24,790
It's also like an open-source site project of Quadrron.

766
00:51:24,790 --> 00:51:27,670
You can find it on our GitHub repositories.

767
00:51:27,670 --> 00:51:35,349
And although like Rox DB is a fantastic general purpose product, there latency spikes were unacceptable.

768
00:51:35,349 --> 00:51:37,750
So we had to do that.

769
00:51:37,750 --> 00:51:40,390
And it has similar functionality to to Rox DB,

770
00:51:40,390 --> 00:51:45,430
but it's just a specialized for our specific use case.

771
00:51:45,430 --> 00:51:49,670
So that actually improved the the latency perceived by the users significantly.

772
00:51:49,670 --> 00:51:54,710
You can also find some benchmarks website that that proves that.

773
00:51:54,710 --> 00:51:56,470
So it like definitely that's something that we are really proud of.

774
00:51:56,480 --> 00:52:00,630
and we also kept the back compatibility.

775
00:52:00,640 --> 00:52:04,790
So even though you were using the version that was still using Rox DB,

776
00:52:04,790 --> 00:52:08,950
you can upgrade to the latest one and still expect it to work.

777
00:52:08,950 --> 00:52:13,270
So that was definitely a challenge that we successfully solved in the recent months.

778
00:52:13,280 --> 00:52:16,550
Thanks for sharing that thoughtprovoking example. That's fascinating.

779
00:52:16,550 --> 00:52:21,109
We'll make sure to link in the show notes the blog post.

780
00:52:21,109 --> 00:52:25,430
that you mentioned a moment ago so others can learn about this challenging story and this successful outcome.

781
00:52:25,430 --> 00:52:27,430
As we draw our episode to a conclusion.

782
00:52:27,430 --> 00:52:31,510
I'm wondering if you could comment briefly on the ways in.

783
00:52:31,510 --> 00:52:36,710
which building and testing and evaluating the performance of a vector similarity search engine.

784
00:52:36,710 --> 00:52:41,030
Is different from other types of software systems with which you have experience.

785
00:52:41,040 --> 00:52:42,549
Yeah, definitely.

786
00:52:42,549 --> 00:52:47,589
I think that might be also interesting for those who would like to join quadrant core team.

787
00:52:47,589 --> 00:52:54,549
So I know that the core team works like they always try to keep the development momentum.

788
00:52:54,549 --> 00:52:57,829
So they implement features in small steps from inside out.

789
00:52:57,829 --> 00:53:02,470
so they can merge them into the main branch quickly without having many diverging versions.

790
00:53:02,480 --> 00:53:06,950
It also makes the reviews and collaboration easier.

791
00:53:06,950 --> 00:53:12,550
So in case of vector databases, I think like testing is key, but we try not to overdo it.

792
00:53:12,559 --> 00:53:17,829
It's not only to prove that a particular feature works now,

793
00:53:17,829 --> 00:53:20,710
but we also try to prove that it won't break in the future.

794
00:53:20,720 --> 00:53:22,710
when we decide to change something.

795
00:53:22,710 --> 00:53:29,030
We also try to handle all the common cases in end to end tests.

796
00:53:29,030 --> 00:53:32,230
And also try to keep the test code minimal.

797
00:53:32,230 --> 00:53:37,030
So it's not like 10 times bigger than the code for the feature itself.

798
00:53:37,030 --> 00:53:39,109
And in our case benchmarking is really hard.

799
00:53:39,109 --> 00:53:45,030
Because you can't really benchmark like an individual feature on some artificial data sets.

800
00:53:45,030 --> 00:53:47,510
But you really need to think about real world use cases.

801
00:53:47,510 --> 00:53:51,990
The good thing is that we have lots of users already.

802
00:53:51,990 --> 00:53:57,349
So we can also build our test cases based on that and our benchmarks too.

803
00:53:57,349 --> 00:54:01,670
So we also like started to recommend doing some custom benchmarks once the requirements are clear.

804
00:54:01,670 --> 00:54:05,670
Cuz there are so many diverse way of how people can use vector search.

805
00:54:05,670 --> 00:54:08,390
And that's something that we that we've learned.

806
00:54:08,390 --> 00:54:14,150
And building distributed systems is really hard.

807
00:54:14,150 --> 00:54:16,230
That's where we struggled a lot and.

808
00:54:16,230 --> 00:54:18,470
yeah I think we are just getting better in building them even.

809
00:54:18,470 --> 00:54:21,510
though our our core team is really not that big.

810
00:54:21,520 --> 00:54:23,349
Thanks for that response.

811
00:54:23,349 --> 00:54:26,790
Now we've talked a lot in this episode about different key concepts.

812
00:54:26,790 --> 00:54:30,230
So we talked about vector embeddings and similarity search.

813
00:54:30,230 --> 00:54:35,349
And we've gone through many of the details about both how you use Quadrant.

814
00:54:35,349 --> 00:54:37,829
But then also about how you actually have gone to build quadrant.

815
00:54:37,829 --> 00:54:42,950
or to do the benchmarking associated with quadrant. at the very end of our discussion.

816
00:54:42,950 --> 00:54:48,231
Now, I'm wondering if you could comment briefly on what you see as like the future of vector. databases.

817
00:54:48,231 --> 00:54:52,790
And their overall role in what we might call the AI or machine learning landscape.

818
00:54:52,800 --> 00:54:56,950
Well, definitely vector databases are not dead.

819
00:54:56,950 --> 00:55:01,829
Even though I've seen like multiple posts on LinkedIn about like the end of of the whole industry.

820
00:55:01,829 --> 00:55:04,470
The main problem of of AI or LLMs,

821
00:55:04,470 --> 00:55:09,829
I know that we use these two terms are terms as synonyms.

822
00:55:09,829 --> 00:55:11,829
But even the latest LLM can suffer from knowledge cutoff.

823
00:55:11,829 --> 00:55:18,549
Because they were trained and on some specific data sets and definitely don't know the most recent news.

824
00:55:18,549 --> 00:55:20,790
And none of them could have been trained on your own data.

825
00:55:20,790 --> 00:55:29,990
So definitely some sort of retrieval is needed and vector databases will definitely serve that functionality for them.

826
00:55:29,990 --> 00:55:35,670
Because semantic search is just so well suited for natural language like search or multimodel rack,

827
00:55:35,670 --> 00:55:40,309
because that's also something that started to be implemented recently.

828
00:55:40,309 --> 00:55:47,430
And vector search is not only important in terms of rack or llms,

829
00:55:47,430 --> 00:55:52,630
but nearest neighbor search is just such a versatile method that can solve multiple problems.

830
00:55:52,630 --> 00:55:56,710
That I still feel that it's too early to say like what are what would be the typical.

831
00:55:56,710 --> 00:55:58,710
use cases in the upcoming two or three years.

832
00:55:58,720 --> 00:56:05,190
But I feel like many of us will start implementing something more than just which we've alumented generation.

833
00:56:05,190 --> 00:56:13,190
And we will definitely see the applications of vector search for example as some sort of guard rails.

834
00:56:13,190 --> 00:56:16,470
before we input any data into NLM.

835
00:56:16,470 --> 00:56:21,270
Because we can perform anomaly detection with nearest neighbor search easily.

836
00:56:21,270 --> 00:56:23,589
And I'm looking forward to seeing some new use cases for.

837
00:56:23,589 --> 00:56:25,270
that and pretty sure that's definitely going to happen.

838
00:56:25,280 --> 00:56:31,190
Okay, that makes sense and I can say from my own experience associated with using Quadrant,

839
00:56:31,190 --> 00:56:35,430
it is flexible and it can handle a wide variety of different documents.

840
00:56:35,430 --> 00:56:37,589
So I do think it's an area where there's still a lot of growth.

841
00:56:37,589 --> 00:56:41,510
And the point that you made previously was a good one in regards to the fact.

842
00:56:41,510 --> 00:56:46,230
that you often want your own standalone system for similarity search.

843
00:56:46,230 --> 00:56:50,230
So that you can let the database that's relational do what it's good at.

844
00:56:50,230 --> 00:56:52,390
And then have another system that can do what it's good at.

845
00:56:52,390 --> 00:56:57,829
So with all of those points in mind and the thoughtful insights that you've shared so far.

846
00:56:57,829 --> 00:57:01,589
Are there any additional topics that we didn't cover that you think we should briefly discuss?

847
00:57:01,589 --> 00:57:05,349
I think we should mention the importance of evaluation.

848
00:57:05,349 --> 00:57:12,870
That's something that people tend to ignore when they build retrieval augmented generation or vector search in general.

849
00:57:12,870 --> 00:57:15,670
However, retrieval or search is not a new topic.

850
00:57:15,670 --> 00:57:20,950
We've been discussing proper ways to to do retrieval and evaluate it for ages.

851
00:57:20,950 --> 00:57:27,911
And even though you may choose the best performing embedding model from the public leaderboards. Or choose the best LLM.

852
00:57:27,920 --> 00:57:31,910
Your system may just struggle with your specific data.

853
00:57:31,910 --> 00:57:36,630
Because none of these models was trained on something that would resemble it.

854
00:57:36,630 --> 00:57:42,309
So unless you are experimenting and I don't know doing like a side project over a weekend.

855
00:57:42,309 --> 00:57:51,190
It's always a good idea to start your semantic search journey with building well curated ground data set.

856
00:57:51,190 --> 00:57:53,910
that will serve as a quality judge.

857
00:57:53,910 --> 00:57:56,630
So you can see whether your retrieval is really doing a great job.

858
00:57:56,640 --> 00:57:59,991
Thanks for that comment about evaluation. That makes a lot of sense.

